Table users {
  user_id int [pk, increment]
  first_name varchar(50) [not null]
  last_name varchar(50) [not null]
  email varchar(120) [not null, unique]
  password_hash varchar(255) [not null]
  phone varchar(20)
  role varchar(20) [not null]
  created_at datetime [not null]
  updated_at datetime [not null]
}
Table users {
  user_id int [pk, increment]
  first_name varchar(50) [not null]
  last_name varchar(50) [not null]
  email varchar(120) [not null, unique]
  password_hash varchar(255) [not null]
  phone varchar(20)
  role varchar(20) [not null, note: 'Organizer | Participant | Admin']
  created_at datetime [not null]
  updated_at datetime [not null]
}

Table participant_profiles {
  participant_id int [pk, increment]
  user_id int [not null, unique, ref: > users.user_id]
  date_of_birth date
  gender varchar(20)
  emergency_contact_name varchar(100)
  emergency_contact_phone varchar(20)
  club_name varchar(100)
  created_at datetime [not null]
  updated_at datetime [not null]
}

Table organizer_profiles {
  organizer_id int [pk, increment]
  user_id int [not null, unique, ref: > users.user_id]
  organization_name varchar(120) [not null]
  organization_type varchar(50)
  bio text
  verified bit [not null, default: 0]
  created_at datetime [not null]
  updated_at datetime [not null]
}
# ERD Notes (Section A)

## Core Entities
- users
- participant_profiles
- organizer_profiles
- events
- categories
- enrollments
- results

## Relationship Summary
- One user can have one participant profile.
- One user can have one organizer profile.
- One organizer can create many events.
- One event can have many categories.
- Participants and categories are many-to-many via enrollments.
- Each enrollment can have one result.

## Constraint Decisions
- Email and enrollment unique codes are unique.
- Enrollment prevents duplicates using (participant_id, category_id).
- Result is unique per enrollment.
